<http://villainscope.org/villain/villain_movie_0107> <http://villainscope.org/villain/personalityDetails> "Music: Leitmotif. Symbolic Tune - Beckert incessantly whistles Edvard Grieg's \"In the Hall of the Mountain King\". This specific piece is symbolic, as it originally accompanied a scene where trolls sing about slaughtering the protagonist. The Trigger - The whistling functions as a nervous \"tic\" and a psychological \"trigger\" that awakens Beckert’s uncontrollable side shortly before he commits an atrocity. Evidence of Lack of Control -This musical leitmotif serves as a key piece of evidence that his personality is \"evenly bisected\" and that he cannot govern his own behavior, which ultimately leads the audience to take pity on him. | Sympathetic Villain: He is depicted as a man suffering from intense pain and anguish because he kills against his will. By portraying him as a pathetic victim of his own impulses, Lang forces the audience to feel empathy for a man who would normally be considered a monster. | Dual Personality: Reveal the \"torments of his soul\" and the internal struggle between his stable self and the \"demons\" that drive him to kill." .
